{
  "image_name": "4e688092e2b4ab90e8b835596bafb4d0.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how bone marrow stem cells give rise to the main bone-forming and bone-resorbing cell types, while highlighting genes and signaling pathways that influence these fates. Bone marrow is shown as the source of both haematopoietic stem cells and mesenchymal stem cells. The haematopoietic branch produces immune-related cells and, through monocytes/macrophages, generates osteoclast progenitors, pre-osteoclasts, and mature osteoclasts, with apoptosis as an alternative outcome. The mesenchymal branch produces osteoblast precursors, pre-osteoblasts, osteoblasts, bone lining cells, and osteocytes, while also branching toward myocytes and adipocytes.\n\nSeveral labeled factors appear to promote or inhibit key transitions. For example, ORLNC1 seems to inhibit mesenchymal stem cell differentiation toward osteoblast precursors, whereas ESR1 appears to promote this route and also supports osteoclast survival. Other genes, including ANXA2, EN1, and DAAM2, are placed near later differentiation steps, suggesting regulatory roles in osteoclast or osteocyte development.\n\nOn the right, a signaling schematic inside an osteoblast emphasizes WNT/β-catenin signaling through Frizzled and LRP5, leading to gene regulation and bone formation. This pathway is negatively regulated by sclerostin and DKK1, with sclerostin linked to SOST expression in osteocytes and modulation by PTH/PTHR1.",
